<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Herman Melville</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1819">1819</a>–<a href="/years/1891">1891</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Herman Melvill</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Herman Melville was an American writer of the American <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> period, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> City in 1819. He went to sea in 1839 and in 1841 sailed on the whaler Acushnet, jumped ship in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marquesas-islands">Marquesas Islands</a>, and drew on his encounters with the islanders for his first books, Typee (1846) and Omoo (1847). Moby-Dick (1851) found no audience, and after publishing short fiction such as Benito Cereno in magazines he turned to poetry and worked as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> customs inspector in New York. Little known when he died in 1891, he was the subject of a revival from the centennial of his birth in 1919, and Moby-Dick came to be considered one of the Great American Novels.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/herman-melville.jpg" alt="Herman Melville" width="330" height="407" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joseph Oriel Eaton · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herman_Melville_by_Joseph_O_Eaton.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Melville was the third of eight children of Allan Melvill, a commission merchant and importer of French dry goods, and Maria Gansevoort Melvill. Both his grandfathers played roles in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a>: Major Thomas Melvill took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston-tea-party">Boston Tea Party</a>, and General Peter Gansevoort commanded the defence of Fort Stanwix in 1777. Allan's death in 1832 left the family in dire financial straits and increased the influence of his mother's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calvinism">Calvinism</a>. Melville worked as a clerk, attended the Albany Classical School and the Albany Academy, and after the Panic of 1837 taught at Sikes District School near Lenox, Massachusetts, where he had about 30 pupils.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Typee, finished in the summer of 1845, was published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in February 1846 by John Murray and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> on 17 March by Wiley &amp; Putnam, becoming an overnight bestseller. Mardi (1849) was not well received, and Redburn and White-Jacket did not sell well enough to support his growing family. Melville told Richard Henry Dana Jr. in a letter of 1 May 1850 that he was halfway through Moby-Dick, and that summer he read Thomas Carlyle while transforming his plan for the book. In September 1850 he borrowed three thousand dollars from his father-in-law to buy a farm at Pittsfield.</p><p>Moby-Dick did not find an audience, and Pierre (1852) was attacked by the New York Day Book under the headline &quot;HERMAN MELVILLE CRAZY&quot;. From 1853 to 1856 he published short fiction, including &quot;Bartleby, the Scrivener&quot; and &quot;Benito Cereno&quot;. He lectured from late 1857 to 1860, was panned by audiences, and in 1863 moved to a house at 104 East 26th Street in New York. His Battle-Pieces appeared in 1866 and the epic Clarel in 1876, and he retired on 31 December 1885.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In his last years Melville privately published two volumes of poems, John Marr and Other Sailors (1888) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timoleon">Timoleon</a> (1891), each in a print run of 25 copies. He died on the morning of 28 September 1891, and his death certificate gives &quot;cardiac dilation&quot; as the cause. He is buried at Woodlawn Cemetery in the Bronx.</p><p>Over his lifetime his writings earned him just over ten thousand dollars, and by 1876 all his books were out of print. The revival began in 1917 with Carl Van Doren's article on him; Raymond Weaver wrote the first full-length biography in 1921 and edited the unfinished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of Billy Budd, published in 1924. The Library of America began issuing his works in 1982, and in 1985 the corner of Park Avenue South and 26th Street was dedicated as Herman Melville Square.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Herman Melville” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Harper and Brothers published <b>Herman Melville</b>'s Moby-Dick, a month after its London edition.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-14">14 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
